\value{
A \code{gllvmTMB} object. With no covariance-structure terms in
the formula the result has class \code{"gllvmTMB"} (single-response
engine); with \code{latent()} or other covstruct sugar it has class
\code{c("gllvmTMB_multi", "gllvmTMB")} (multi-trait engine).
S3 methods are registered on the \strong{subclasses}, not on bare
\code{gllvmTMB}: for a multi-trait fit (\code{gllvmTMB_multi}), \code{tidy()},
\code{predict()}, \code{summary()}, \code{confint()} and \code{logLik()} are available, and
\code{AIC()} / \code{BIC()} dispatch through \code{logLik()} only when the fitted point is
an unpenalised native-Laplace ML maximum. AGHQ uses a different integration
objective and a loading ridge returns a penalised MAP point, so ordinary
likelihood comparisons do not apply. An MSPL result has leading class
\code{"gllvmTMB_mspl"}; likelihood comparison, standard errors, intervals,
profiles, and hypothesis-test methods fail closed because this experimental
surface is point estimation only. Trait-level extractors such as
\code{extract_ICC_site()} and \code{extract_communality()} are multi-trait only.

\code{vcov()} and \code{coef()} are available too — see
\link{gllvmTMB_multi-vcov}. \code{coef()} works on any fit; \code{vcov()} needs the
\code{sdreport()} and raises the same typed errors \code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} does when it
is missing or non-finite.

\emph{History, 2026-08-04:} this block used to say \code{vcov()} dispatched on
\code{gllvmTMB}, which was \strong{never true of any release} — it and \code{coef()}
existed only for \code{gllvmTMB_va}, where they refuse. The wording was
corrected first, then the two methods were added, which is why the
promise now holds.
}
\description{
Top-level entry point for models with several responses per site,
individual, species, or study. Start from a wide data frame with
\code{\link[=traits]{traits()}} on the formula left-hand side, or from already-stacked long
data with one row per \verb{(unit, trait)} observation. Both routes estimate
the same trait covariance, pairwise correlations, shared latent axes,
and trait-specific variance. The formula syntax also supports fixed
effects plus covariance-structure keywords organised by
\emph{correlation source} (none / animal / phylo / spatial / kernel) and
three \emph{modes} (independent / dependent / latent). The \code{common = TRUE}
modifier on \verb{*_indep()} gives the one-shared-variance special case:
}
\details{
\tabular{llll}{
\strong{source \\ mode} \tab \strong{independent} \tab \strong{dependent} \tab \strong{latent} \cr
\emph{none}    \tab \code{\link[=indep]{indep()}}         \tab \code{\link[=dep]{dep()}}         \tab \code{\link[=latent]{latent()}}         \cr
\emph{animal}  \tab \code{\link[=animal_indep]{animal_indep()}}  \tab \code{\link[=animal_dep]{animal_dep()}}  \tab \code{\link[=animal_latent]{animal_latent()}}  \cr
\emph{phylo}   \tab \code{\link[=phylo_indep]{phylo_indep()}}   \tab \code{\link[=phylo_dep]{phylo_dep()}}   \tab \code{\link[=phylo_latent]{phylo_latent()}}   \cr
\emph{spatial} \tab \code{\link[=spatial_indep]{spatial_indep()}} \tab \code{\link[=spatial_dep]{spatial_dep()}} \tab \code{\link[=spatial_latent]{spatial_latent()}} \cr
\emph{kernel}  \tab \code{\link[=kernel_indep]{kernel_indep()}}  \tab \code{\link[=kernel_dep]{kernel_dep()}}  \tab \code{\link[=kernel_latent]{kernel_latent()}}  \cr
}

The three covariance modes (\code{indep} / \code{dep} / \code{latent}) encode
covstruct intent across traits:
\itemize{
\item \code{latent} — the \strong{decomposition} mode
\eqn{\boldsymbol\Sigma = \boldsymbol\Lambda \boldsymbol\Lambda^\top + \boldsymbol\Psi}:
a low-rank shared part plus a diagonal \eqn{\boldsymbol\Psi} companion.
Ordinary \code{latent()} carries \eqn{\boldsymbol\Psi} by default; the
\verb{unique =} argument controls it (\code{latent(..., unique = FALSE)} for the
loadings-only subset, \verb{*_latent(..., unique = TRUE)} to fold the
\eqn{\boldsymbol\Psi} companion into a source-specific term).
\item \code{indep} — the \strong{marginal-only} mode: each trait gets its own
variance, no cross-trait covariance.
\item \code{dep} — the \strong{full unstructured} mode: \eqn{\boldsymbol\Sigma}
is free with \eqn{T(T+1)/2} parameters via a Cholesky factor.
}

The five Gaussian long-format response-column slope helpers are \code{\link[=slope]{slope()}},
\code{\link[=phylo_slope]{phylo_slope()}}, \code{\link[=animal_slope]{animal_slope()}}, \code{\link[=kernel_slope]{kernel_slope()}}, and \code{\link[=spatial_slope]{spatial_slope()}}.
Their RHS is the resolved response-column factor, so they are not extra
grid modes. Plus \code{\link[=meta_V]{meta_V()}} (known-V meta-analytic; \code{\link[=meta_known_V]{meta_known_V()}} is a
deprecated alias), and the engine-internal \code{propto()} /
\code{equalto()} covstructs (used by the canonical keywords above; not
typically called directly). See the
\href{https://itchyshin.github.io/gllvmTMB/articles/api-keyword-grid.html}{formula keyword grid article}
for a one-paragraph tour of each cell.

Response-column coefficient helpers are also outside the grid.
\code{\link[=column_coef]{column_coef()}} fits IID random intercept/slope bases across response
columns; \code{\link[=phylo_coef]{phylo_coef()}} fits the same bases with fixed or estimated
phylogenetic correlation strength; \code{\link[=animal_coef]{animal_coef()}} fits them from a pedigree,
relationship covariance, or relationship precision with fixed correlation
strength; \code{\link[=kernel_coef]{kernel_coef()}} uses one labelled dense kernel with fixed or
estimated strength; and \code{\link[=spatial_coef]{spatial_coef()}} uses one labelled response-column
mesh with \code{rho = 1}. All five support Gaussian point models in long and
\code{traits(...)} wide form. Non-Gaussian coefficient models, spatial IID
mixtures, and interval inference remain unavailable.

\code{gllvmTMB()} parses the glmmTMB-style formula, converts wide
\code{\link[=traits]{traits()}} input to the same internal stacked-trait representation as
explicit long data, and dispatches to the underlying TMB template.
Covariance-structure terms (\code{latent()}, \code{indep()}, \code{propto()},
\code{equalto()}, \code{spatial()}) are processed by extending the formula parser
and the TMB template.

Per the manuscript, when stacking traits one should set
\code{dispformula = ~ 0} so that no implicit residual variance competes
with structured \verb{indep(0 + trait | …)} terms. \code{gllvmTMB()} enforces
this internally.

\strong{Multi-trial binomial}. The TMB engine evaluates \code{dbinom(y, n_trials, p)}
so binomial fits are not restricted to Bernoulli (size = 1). To pass a
per-row trial count, use either:
\itemize{
\item \code{cbind(successes, failures) ~ ...} on the LHS of the formula
(canonical R / \code{glmmTMB} convention; recommended), or
\item a flat response \code{y} with \code{weights = n_trials} (the alternative
glmmTMB API).
}
Both interfaces produce identical fits. For Bernoulli data, omit
\code{weights} and use a 0/1 response; the engine sets \code{n_trials = 1} and
the likelihood is identical to the previous Bernoulli-only behaviour.

\strong{Missing responses and predictors.} \code{NA} response cells are treated as
unobserved unit-trait cells. Under the default
\code{miss_control(response = "drop")}, they are dropped before the TMB likelihood
is built; with \code{miss_control(response = "include")}, they are kept in the
model data, masked out of the likelihood, and available through
\code{\link[=predict_missing]{predict_missing()}}. For \code{cbind(successes, failures)} binomial responses, a
row is treated as missing when either response component is missing.
Observation weights, when supplied, are subset to retained likelihood rows
before validation. Ordinary missing predictors, grouping variables, or
fixed-effect design values still error because the model cannot construct a
design row; explicitly modelled missing predictors use \code{mi(x)},
\code{miss_control(predictor = "model")}, and \code{impute = list(...)}.
The modelled-\code{mi()} route is native Laplace only; the variational route
refuses \code{mi()} predictors. Dense missing-response masks have their own
narrower VA fence and do not imply predictor-model support.
These contracts are covered by the package's missing-data validation tests,
both for responses and for predictors.

\strong{Delta (hurdle) families.} \code{delta_lognormal()} and \code{delta_gamma()}
use a \emph{single} linear predictor for both components: presence is
\eqn{\Pr(y > 0) = \mathrm{invlogit}(\eta)} and the positive-component
mean is \eqn{E[y \mid y > 0] = \exp(\eta)}. This matches sdmTMB's
standard delta default (one fixed-effects matrix, one set of trait
random effects). The shared-predictor scheme is the simplest hurdle
formulation; a future release may decouple the two predictors so
presence and abundance can have independent fixed and random effects.
Only \code{type = "standard"} (the default) is currently wired in the
multivariate engine; \code{type = "poisson-link"} is not implemented. Each
delta family carries one per-trait dispersion of the \emph{positive}
component (no extra Bernoulli dispersion). The response must be
non-negative.
\subsection{Per-trait residual variance: when does it activate?}{

"Residual" in a mixed-effects model is \strong{scale-relative}: what
counts as residual variance shifts as you add levels to the model.
In a Gaussian fit without a per-row diagonal term, the residual is row-level
noise captured by a within-family shared \code{sigma_eps}. Once you add a per-row
\code{indep(0 + trait | obs)} term,
the \emph{row-level} residual is now T per-trait random-effect variances and
\code{sigma_eps} is auto-suppressed to avoid double-counting. If you also add a
site-level diagonal term on top, the row-level term remains the residual and
the \code{site}-level term is now an additional, \emph{higher-level} random effect, not
a residual at all. The dispatch table below records this explicitly for the
configurations the engine supports today.

Gaussian and lognormal responses each have a residual scale parameter,
\code{sigma_eps}, shared within family. Pure fits retain one slot; a joint
Gaussian-lognormal fit has separate raw- and log-scale slots. Ordinary
Gamma responses instead carry a per-trait shape
\code{phi_gamma} (CV = \code{1 / sqrt(phi_gamma)}). Per-trait residual variances
only appear if you explicitly add a per-row \code{indep(...)} term.
The dispatch is automatic:

\describe{
\item{No per-row \code{indep}, Gaussian/lognormal present}{One slot in a
pure fit; separate Gaussian raw-scale and lognormal log-scale slots when
both coexist. Each slot is shared within family, \emph{not} per-trait.}
\item{No per-row \code{indep}, no Gaussian/lognormal rows}{\code{sigma_eps}
is mapped off; the family's intrinsic dispersion handles the residual
(for ordinary Gamma, \code{phi_gamma}).}
\item{\code{indep(0 + trait | g)} where \code{g} has fewer levels than
rows (e.g. \code{g = "site"})}{\code{sigma_eps} is still estimated as the
row-level residual; the diagonal term adds a per-trait random
effect at level \code{g} on top.}
\item{\code{indep(0 + trait | obs)} at the per-row level (one
level per row), Gaussian/lognormal rows fitted}{\code{sigma_eps} is
auto-suppressed (mapped off, fixed at a tiny stabiliser); the
T per-trait diagonal random effects \emph{are} the residual. A
one-shot \code{cli::cli_inform} fires at fit time announcing the
auto-suppression.}
\item{\code{indep(0 + trait | obs)} at the per-row level,
non-Gaussian or mixed-family fit}{Treated as observation-level
random effects (OLRE). For Bernoulli traits the OLRE is
statistically unidentifiable and is mapped off; for hurdle /
delta families a warning is emitted (see "Per-family-aware OLRE
selection" below).}
}

Mnemonic: Gaussian/lognormal \code{sigma_eps} is the default; ordinary Gamma uses
\code{phi_gamma}; per-row \code{indep} replaces the Gaussian/lognormal family residual
with T per-trait residuals; non-per-row \code{indep} adds a higher-level random
effect on top of the family residual;
non-continuous families never carry \code{sigma_eps} regardless.

\strong{Per-family-aware OLRE selection.} When
\verb{indep(0 + trait | <unit_obs>)} is at
per-row resolution, i.e. one row per \verb{(trait, unit_obs)} cell, the resulting
per-trait random effects on the linear predictor are an observation-level
random effect (OLRE). The engine now decides per trait what to do with the
OLRE variance based on the trait's response family:
\itemize{
\item \strong{single-trial Bernoulli} (\code{binomial()}, all rows have
\code{n_trials == 1}): \code{theta_diag_W[t]} and the corresponding
\code{s_W} column are mapped off. The OLRE is statistically
unidentifiable here (no within-cell replication) and the MLE
is the trivial boundary \eqn{\sigma_W \to 0}; pinning the
parameter at \eqn{\sigma_W \approx 10^{-6}} removes the
spurious free parameter and emits a one-shot informational
message. Multi-trial binomial (via \code{cbind(succ, fail)} or
\code{weights = n_trials}) is identifiable and fit normally.
\item \strong{delta / hurdle families} (\code{delta_lognormal()},
\code{delta_gamma()}): OLRE is fit as before, but a one-shot
warning is emitted because the OLRE acts on the \emph{shared}
linear predictor of the hurdle and mixes presence and
positive-component noise.
\item \strong{all other families}: OLRE is fit as before.
}
In a mixed-family fit (per-row family list) the per-trait decision
applies only to the affected trait. References: Nakagawa &
Schielzeth (2010) \emph{Biol. Rev.} 85: 935-956; Nakagawa, Johnson &
Schielzeth (2017) \emph{J. R. Soc. Interface} 14: 20170213.
}
}
\section{Dispersion parameters in the report slot}{

The fitted object's \verb{$report} carries per-family dispersion quantities on
the natural scale, each a vector of length \code{n_traits} indexed by trait
(except \code{sigma_eps}, which has one slot normally and two slots only when
Gaussian and lognormal responses coexist). Their names follow the engine's
internal parameterisation, \strong{not} standard R distribution arguments —
several are easy to misread. What each one IS, and how it maps to
standard R arguments (\code{mu = exp(eta)} unless noted):\tabular{lll}{
   Family (report name) \tab What it is \tab Standard-R conversion \cr
   gaussian, lognormal (\code{sigma_eps}) \tab residual SD / sdlog; one within-family shared slot in pure fits, or separate Gaussian raw-scale and lognormal log-scale slots when both coexist \tab \code{pnorm(y, mean = eta, sd = sigma_eps[1])}; \code{plnorm(y, meanlog = eta, sdlog = sigma_eps[2])} in a joint fit \cr
   Gamma (\code{phi_gamma}) \tab the \strong{shape}, not a dispersion \tab \code{pgamma(y, shape = phi, scale = mu / phi)}; \code{CV(y) = 1 / sqrt(phi)} \cr
   nbinom2 (\code{phi_nbinom2}) \tab the NB \code{size} \tab \code{pnbinom(y, size = phi, mu = mu)}; \code{Var(y) = mu + mu^2 / phi} \cr
   nbinom1 (\code{phi_nbinom1}) \tab linear overdispersion, \code{Var(y) = mu * (1 + phi)} \tab \code{pnbinom(y, size = mu / phi, mu = mu)} — the size is mean-dependent \cr
   tweedie (\code{phi_tweedie}, \code{p_tweedie}) \tab dispersion and power \code{p} in (1, 2) \tab no base-R CDF; \code{tweedie::ptweedie(y, mu = mu, phi = phi, power = p)} \cr
   Beta (\code{phi_beta}) \tab the precision \tab \code{pbeta(y, shape1 = mu * phi, shape2 = (1 - mu) * phi)} with \code{mu = plogis(eta)} \cr
   betabinomial (\code{phi_betabinom}) \tab precision of the Beta mixing \tab \code{a = mu * phi}, \code{b = (1 - mu) * phi} with \code{mu = plogis(eta)}; no base-R CDF \cr
   student (\code{sigma_student}, \code{df_student}) \tab the \strong{scale}, not the SD \tab \code{pt((y - eta) / sigma, df)}; \code{SD(y) = sigma * sqrt(df / (df - 2))}, undefined for \code{df <= 2} \cr
   truncated_nbinom2 (\code{phi_truncnb2}) \tab the NB \code{size}; a \strong{separate} vector from \code{phi_nbinom2} \tab \code{pnbinom(y, size = phi, mu = mu)} renormalised by \code{1 - pnbinom(0, ...)} \cr
   delta_lognormal (\code{sigma_lognormal_delta}) \tab sdlog of the positive part \tab \code{plnorm(y, meanlog = eta, sdlog = sigma)} for \code{y > 0}; \code{P(y > 0) = plogis(eta)} \cr
   delta_gamma (\code{phi_gamma_delta}) \tab the \strong{CV} of the positive part, not a shape \tab \code{pgamma(y, shape = 1 / phi^2, scale = mu * phi^2)} for \code{y > 0}; \code{P(y > 0) = plogis(eta)} \cr
}
}

\section{Objective provenance}{
 joint_nll report fields vs objective_components:
A fitted object carries two different surfaces that both look like "the
objective", and they answer different questions.

\code{report$joint_nll_unpenalized} and \code{report$joint_nll_penalized} are
\strong{conditional joint} quantities computed on the TMB tape (the negative
log-likelihood of the data and random effects at their current values,
before and after TMB-side MSPL penalty terms are added). They see only
penalties applied \emph{inside} the C++ template -- the LA-MSPL penalties
documented above -- and never see anything computed at the R level.

\code{fit$objective_components} is the \strong{marginal} surface: the value TMB's
Laplace approximation integrates the random effects out of, decomposed
into \code{likelihood_nll} (the unpenalised marginal negative log-likelihood),
\code{ridge_penalty} (the R-level loading ridge from \code{aghq_ridge}, added
\emph{outside} the TMB objective -- see \code{.gllvmTMB_penalised_gradient()}),
\code{optimization_nll} (their sum, the objective the optimiser actually
minimised), and \code{optimizer_reported} (the value \code{nlminb()} / \code{optim()}
returned). This is the \strong{only} place the R-level loading ridge appears;
\verb{report$joint_nll_*} cannot see it because it never reaches the TMB tape.

The two surfaces are \strong{not expected to match}, on a ridged fit or
otherwise, and a difference between them is not itself an error: they are
conditional-joint and marginal quantities from different penalty regimes.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
set.seed(1)
sim <- simulate_site_trait(n_sites = 50, n_species = 8, n_traits = 3,
                           mean_species_per_site = 5)
# Long format: one response value per site-species-trait row.
fit_long <- gllvmTMB(
  value ~ 0 + trait + (0 + trait):env_1 + (0 + trait):env_2,
  data   = sim$data,
  family = gaussian(),
  trait  = "trait",
  unit   = "site_species"
)

# Wide format: one row per site-species, with the same three traits.
# `traits()` expands `1 + env_1 + env_2` to the explicit long terms above.
wide_values <- stats::reshape(
  sim$data[c("site_species", "trait", "value")],
  idvar = "site_species", timevar = "trait", direction = "wide"
)
wide <- merge(
  unique(sim$data[c("site_species", "env_1", "env_2")]), wide_values,
  by = "site_species", sort = FALSE
)
names(wide) <- sub("^value[.]", "", names(wide))
fit_wide <- gllvmTMB(
  traits(trait_1, trait_2, trait_3) ~ 1 + env_1 + env_2,
  data   = wide,
  family = gaussian(),
  unit   = "site_species"
)
summary(fit_long)
summary(fit_wide)
}
}
\references{
Sterzinger, P. and Kosmidis, I. (2023). Maximum softly-penalized
likelihood for mixed effects logistic regression. \emph{Statistics and
Computing}, 33, 53. \doi{10.1007/s11222-023-10217-3}.
This paper is the logistic mixed-effects antecedent for the fixed-effect
Jeffreys component. The probit, complementary-log-log, reduced-rank GLLVM,
and spatial penalties implemented here are gllvmTMB-specific extensions;
that paper does not validate those extensions.
}
